 de Realimentación\"\" (Refeeding Syndrome) is a serious clinical condition that can arise in patients who have experienced severe malnutrition and are subsequently reintroduced to nutritional support. This syndrome is characterized by a range of metabolic complications that can occur when nutrients, particularly carbohydrates, are administered too quickly. It poses significant health risks, including severe electrolyte imbalances, arrhythmias, and even coma, making careful monitoring and management essential.\n\nPatients at risk for this condition include those who are malnourished, especially after periods of fasting or following surgical procedures. The reintroduction of feeding must be approached with caution, as the sudden influx of calories can lead to dangerous metabolic disturbances. The condition is particularly associated with thiamine deficiency and can manifest as a potentially fatal complication if not properly identified and managed.\n\nIn summary, the Síndrome de Realimentación is a critical medical condition that necessitates vigilant oversight during the refeeding process in malnourished individuals to prevent severe health complications.\",16\r\n2189,ALTERACIONES ELECTROLÍTICAS,\"Alteraciones Electrolíticas refer to disturbances in electrolyte balance that can occur in patients receiving nutritional support, significantly impacting their overall health. These imbalances are particularly common in critically ill patients, making careful nutritional management essential to restore and maintain electrolyte balance. Dietary plans are designed to prevent potential health issues associated with these electrolyte imbalances, highlighting the importance of monitoring and addressing this aspect of patient care.\",2\r\n2075,ALTERACIONES HIDROELECTROLÍTICAS,\"ALTERACIONES HIDROELECTROLÍTICAS refer to disturbances in the levels of electrolytes in the body, which can lead to serious health issues, particularly in malnourished patients. These imbalances often necessitate careful monitoring and management to prevent complications and ensure the well-being of affected individuals.\",2\r\n15500,PACIENTE PEDIÁTRICO,\"The entity \"\"PACIENTE PEDIÁTRICO\"\" refers to children and adolescents who may require medical attention, particularly in the context of nutritional support. These patients often receive parenteral nutrition due to various medical conditions that put them at risk for metabolic and nutritional complications. Parenteral nutrition is a critical intervention for pediatric patients who cannot obtain adequate nutrition through enteral means.\",13\r\n2184,FRA,\"Acute renal failure (FRA) is a condition that affects the kidneys' ability to filter waste from the blood, requiring specific nutritional interventions.\",8\r\n2081,ENFERMEDAD METABÓLICA ÓSEA,\"\"\"ENFERMEDAD METABÓLICA ÓSEA\"\" refers to a condition affecting bone metabolism, particularly prevalent in patients receiving long-term parenteral nutrition. This condition is characterized by osteoporosis and osteomalacia, leading to symptoms such as bone pain and an increased risk of fractures due to nutrient deficiencies. It encompasses various bone metabolic disorders that arise as a result of prolonged nutritional support via parenteral means.\",3\r\n8605,ALTERACIONES HEPÁTICAS,\"Alteraciones hepáticas refers to liver disorders that encompass a range of liver abnormalities. These disorders are significant as they serve as a precaution for the use of certain medications, specifically fentermina and topiramato. Additionally, in pediatric patients receiving parenteral nutrition, alteraciones hepáticas can manifest as elevated liver enzymes and cholestasis, highlighting the importance of monitoring liver function in these individuals.\",2\r\n2188,CATABOLISMO PROTEICO,\"CATABOLISMO PROTEICO refers to the metabolic process in which proteins are broken down into amino acids. This process is particularly significant in the context of critically ill patients, as it can be increased under such conditions, thereby impacting their nutritional needs. Understanding catabolismo proteico is essential for managing the dietary requirements of patients, especially those who are critically ill, as their protein breakdown rates may necessitate adjustments in their nutritional intake.\",2\r\n2057,ALIMENTACIÓN PARENTERAL,\"ALIMENTACIÓN PARENTERAL es un método de nutrición que se administra por vía intravenosa. Este tratamiento se utiliza en pacientes que no pueden recibir alimentos por vía oral o enteral, proporcionando así la nutrición necesaria de manera efectiva.\",2\r\n10088,INSULINORRESISTENCIA,\"Insulin resistance is a condition characterized by the body's cells failing to respond effectively to insulin. This condition can complicate the management of patients with chronic kidney disease, as it impacts their nutritional needs. Additionally, insulin resistance may be exacerbated by surgical stress, further complicating the overall health and treatment of affected individuals.\",1\r\n2071,SYNDROME DE REALIMENTACION,\"The syndrome of realimentation occurs in severely malnourished patients undergoing intensive nutritional replenishment, leading to various metabolic complications.\",1\r\n8942,PACIENTES CON AN,Los pacientes con anorexia nerviosa (AN) son aquellos que sufren de desnutrición y requieren un manejo cuidadoso durante la realimentación para evitar complicaciones.,1\r\n15610,DEFICIT DE VITAMINAS,Vitamin deficiencies can occur in patients receiving parenteral nutrition if not properly supplemented.,1\r\n15611,FRACTURA INTESTINAL,\"Intestinal failure can lead to complications in patients requiring long-term parenteral nutrition, affecting nutrient absorption.\",1\r\n2186,HIPERTIGLICERIDEMIA,\"Hypertriglyceridemia is a condition characterized by high levels of triglycerides in the blood, which can complicate the nutritional status of critically ill patients.\",1\r\n2187,VITAMINAS HIDROSOLUBLES,\"Water-soluble vitamins are essential nutrien